From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:45:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: take a whole-refresh picture offset into the frame
 latency where a frame spans several refreshes

In frame-sequential 3D each film frame is on screen for four refreshes,
and the picture settled a whole refresh late: +10.4 ms at 96 Hz on every
start. The genlock places the picture inside one refresh period and so
cannot see a whole one; in 2D a whole refresh is a frame, and moving by
it is not wanted, but here it is a quarter of one. The audio trim walked
it out in about twenty seconds instead.

Once the pipeline count has settled, an alignment that stays a whole
number of refreshes off for about two seconds is taken out of the frame
latency for the rate, clock untouched, once per start, and kept for the
next start at that rate, which then lands there from its first picture.
More than two refreshes in all is no placement this can mend, and turns
it off for the rate.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
@@ -116,6 +116,39 @@
   m_rearm.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed);
 }
 
+void CAMLLatency::TrackRefreshShift(double alignment)
+{
+  // Seen this many frames running before it is believed: about two seconds.
+  constexpr unsigned int SHIFT_AFTER_FRAMES = 48;
+
+  // Settled only: until then the count itself can be a refresh out. And only
+  // where a frame spans whole refreshes: elsewhere a refresh is a frame.
+  const int k = m_period > 0.0 ? static_cast<int>(std::lround(alignment / m_period)) : 0;
+  if (!m_settled || m_shiftDone || aml_refreshes_per_frame() <= 1.0 || k == 0 ||
+      std::abs(alignment - k * m_period) > m_period / 4.0)
+  {
+    m_shiftRun = 0;
+    return;
+  }
+  if (m_shiftRun == 0 || k != m_shiftRefreshes)
+  {
+    m_shiftRefreshes = k;
+    m_shiftRun = 0;
+  }
+  if (++m_shiftRun < SHIFT_AFTER_FRAMES)
+    return;
+
+  m_shiftRun = 0;
+  m_shiftDone = true;
+  // Frame latency moves the picture the same way as itself: a picture late by
+  // k refreshes needs k less.
+  CAMLLatencyStore::GetInstance().Shift(DVD_TIME_BASE / m_period, -k);
+  // The picture moves against the audio, as a placement does.
+  NoteClockDisturbed();
+  CLog::Log(LOGINFO, "CAMLLatency: picture {} refresh(es) late, frame latency now {:+.1f}ms over it",
+            k, CAMLLatencyStore::GetInstance().GetShift());
+}
